WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.208
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:10.808
In California, whenever I want to
start a conversation with someone

00:00:10.808 --> 00:00:15.930
I have just met, one question I
always ask is, what do you do?

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:23.813
I often get one line responses like,
I am a consultant, or I am an engineer.

00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:29.710
On a recent trip back home in Ghana,
I started a similar conversation

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.330
on the four-hour bus ride from Accra,
the capital, to Kumasi, where I come from.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.300
I started a conversation with Kwame,
the gentleman seated next to me.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.349
What do you do, I asked.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.180
He gave me a paragraph of
information in response.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.390
In fact, I believe he gave me
a chapter worth of details,

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.250
just on the topic of his occupation.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.681
It went like this, I am a lecturer, but

00:00:59.681 --> 00:01:03.010
I also operate a car wash
business on the part time.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:09.548
I have recently discovered an arbitrage
in buying fish from the Futaa region and

00:01:09.548 --> 00:01:12.563
selling in Kumasi for
50% profit margin and

00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:15.360
my wife is helping me expand our business.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.692
For next 30 minute of our ride,

00:01:18.692 --> 00:01:23.796
Kwame went on to discuss the numerous
business opportunities he is thinking

00:01:23.796 --> 00:01:29.000
of perusing from exporting cashew to
Germany, and processing shea butter.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.571
He ended up by expressing his interest
in quitting his job as a lecturer to

00:01:35.571 --> 00:01:38.271
focus on entrepreneurship full time.

00:01:38.271 --> 00:01:44.460
At this time I feared for him, so
I proceeded to tell him why he would fail.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.120
I used every entrepreneur framework
I have learned at Stanford,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.845
carefully telling him to
focus on one opportunity.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:56.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Afterwards, I took a deep breath,

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.310
and we both fell in silence for
the rest of the four hour journey.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.860
As I sat in silence,
I looked out the window and

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.990
I saw the dozens of hawkers that would
rush towards our bus at every bus stop,

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:16.520
selling us everything from toothpaste
to snacks to phone recharge cards.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.699
And I began to fall into
the reality of where I was,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.880
this is Africa that was the decent
amount of the population

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.960
don't wake up every single morning not
quite sure of the deal of the day.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.860
If it is too hot you saw iced water,

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:40.260
if it is raining you saw umbrellas, and
if it's Christmas time you sell cookies.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.305
You don't need a business address for

00:02:43.305 --> 00:02:46.720
my business registration or
any formal training.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.700
You just need logical
strategies of survival.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.090
It wasn't just about where I was.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:59.692
What I saw looking out the window, on
a seven hour journey in the Philippines.

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:05.140
Wasn't different from what I saw on
a two hour train ride in India and

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.250
a seven hour bus trip in Mexico.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.330
And it wasn't different from
what I was seeing in Ghana.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.285
Kwame's intro revealed to me
the fabric of the local economy which

00:03:16.285 --> 00:03:18.909
I completely ignored in giving him advice.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.760
Our conversation was about
the informal economy.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.500
The term informal economy refers to
any economic activity that is in law,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.310
or in practice not sufficiently
covered by formal arrangements.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.890
There are 2 billion people
employed in the informal economy,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.590
which is 60% of the world's
working population.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.309
And it's mostly present in
developing countries, but

00:03:44.309 --> 00:03:46.050
it exists everywhere else in the world.

00:03:49.259 --> 00:03:52.359
In advanced economies like the US and
Europe,

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:56.900
humanity is grappling with a fear
of AI and robotics technologies.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.400
And the question on everyone's mind is,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.850
how can we adapt to this
talent mobility revolution?

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.860
My answer is,
learn about the informal economy.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.410
It is a place in the world where
people are experts in embracing

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.289
an artist in navigating
life's difficulties.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:24.010
I believe learning about the informal
economic is helpful in preparing us

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.610
towards the future,
where most of us would be self employed.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:36.120
Yes, currently, 162 million people who
are self employed in the US and in Europe.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:43.280
For some of them, it is out of choice but
for others it out of necessity.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.468
Which ever your position is, here are
three skills you can learn from people who

00:04:47.468 --> 00:04:50.906
are working in the informal economy
to help you better prepare for

00:04:50.906 --> 00:04:51.989
the future of work.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.589
The first skill is adaptability.

00:04:58.456 --> 00:05:03.439
The Kejetia market is the largest
open air market in West Africa,

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:06.539
and it is located in my home town Kumasi.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:11.620
It has 45,000 stalls,
made out of concrete floors,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.209
wooden slabs, and aluminum roofing sheets.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.740
With a wide variety of
business activities,

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.880
it faces at least three major
fire outbreaks every year.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:28.360
After every fire, store owners whirl,
count their losses, but

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.420
go back to work the next
day like nothing happened.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:39.220
On one such occasion, I visited a market
two days after a fire gutted 150 stores.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.090
I was standing right in the spot,
but to my surprise,

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.920
there was no sign of the fire.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.210
Rather, fresh paint,
and business as usual.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.130
There are so many shocks in
the informal economy, but fear and

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.100
uncertainty isn't one of them.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.620
To move forward in terms of disruption,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.090
we would need to learn how to
embrace chaos in order to adapt.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:11.280
The second skill I would like
to share is diversification.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.239
One thing that surprises
me about living in the US

00:06:17.239 --> 00:06:21.598
is most people tend to focus
pretty much on one career.

00:06:21.598 --> 00:06:27.300
I come from a culture where almost
everyone has a side hustle or a side gig.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.510
The concept of a side hustle
is having a blend of jobs.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.400
It's like having a full time job and
a part time job or

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.990
having multiple independent jobs.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:42.820
Just like the street hawker switches
from selling ice water to umbrellas,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.700
just by studying the weather,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.910
having a diversified career is helpful
in adjusting to shifts in the market.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.710
One thing that is very addictive,

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.200
which we talk little about
is the concept of a salary.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.929
If you have an opportunity to generate
multiple streams of income, take it,

00:07:01.929 --> 00:07:03.499
it changes how you think.

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:11.300
The last scale I would like to discuss,
is relationships.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.490
The informal economy is
a large network of people,

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:21.290
not connected by business class but
by trust.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.618
My mother introduces herself
as a business woman, period.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.873
But let me provide you more details,
she has a 9 to 5 job as

00:07:31.873 --> 00:07:37.130
a high school administrator and
a portfolio of side hassles.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.977
As a kid I remember she used
to buy bale of clothes and

00:07:46.977 --> 00:07:51.920
distribution a small
lots to fashion shops.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.190
She later went on into
coconut oil processing.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.040
And she had to develop
relationships with food chains and

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.610
restaurants to sell to them.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.950
Seven years ago,
she came up with an excellent idea.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.710
She wanted to travel to China
to manufacture products and

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:12.670
import into Ghana.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.240
It was crazy,

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.130
aside the fact that she wasn't quite
sure what she would sell this time.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.970
She didn't even have a passport, but

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.940
just like that, my mother graduated
into an international business woman.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:33.400
For someone with a full-time job, didn't
even own a retail store or a warehouse,

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:38.950
all her successes came from phone calls,
and developing the right relationships.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:48.531
People in the informal economy have three
skills we could all use adaptability,

00:08:48.531 --> 00:08:52.330
diversification, and
relationship building.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.725
Research suggests that by next year,
50% of the US workforce will be

00:08:59.725 --> 00:09:06.030
self-employed working as independent
contractors or independent workers.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.578
I employ you to think forward.

00:09:09.578 --> 00:09:17.300
It is 2035 and
we are leaving in the disruptive economy.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.709
We meet somewhere and
I ask you, what do you do?

00:09:26.639 --> 00:09:28.429
How would you introduce yourself?

00:09:30.327 --> 00:09:31.297
Thank you.

00:09:31.297 --> 00:09:36.962
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:36.962 --> 00:09:39.314
[MUSIC]

